A Promise has exactly three states: **pending** (initial), **fulfilled** (settled with a value), **rejected** (settled with a reason). Once it leaves pending, it can never change state again — a Promise that's already fulfilled ignores any later attempt to reject it, and vice versa.

```javascript
const p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve(42), 100);
});
p.then((value) => console.log(value)); // 42, once, after ~100ms
```

"Settled" is the umbrella term for either fulfilled or rejected — the guarantee that matters is **settle-once**: no `.then` callback ever fires twice for the same promise, and no promise can flip from fulfilled to rejected after the fact. That immutability is what makes promises safe to hand to multiple consumers without any of them racing.

```javascript
async function getUserPostCount(id) {
  const user = await fetchUser(id);
  const posts = await fetchPosts(user.id);
  return posts.length;
}

// is roughly equivalent to:
function getUserPostCount(id) {
  return fetchUser(id)
    .then((user) => fetchPosts(user.id))
    .then((posts) => posts.length);
}
```

An `async function` **always returns a Promise**, even if the body has no `await` at all — returning a plain value from it is equivalent to `Promise.resolve(value)`. Each `await` is a `.then()` in disguise: it suspends the function, schedules the rest as a microtask once the awaited promise settles, and unwraps the resolved value directly into a variable instead of a callback parameter. Nothing here is a new concurrency primitive — it's the same promise chain, written to read top-to-bottom.

```javascript
// .then/.catch style
fetchUser(id)
  .then((user) => fetchPosts(user.id))
  .catch((err) => console.error("failed:", err));

// async/await style — same failure handling, different syntax
async function getPosts(id) {
  try {
    const user = await fetchUser(id);
    return await fetchPosts(user.id);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("failed:", err);
  }
}
```

A rejected `await` throws synchronously **inside** the `async` function, which is exactly why `try`/`catch` — a synchronous-looking construct — works to catch it: `await` is unwrapping the rejection into a real thrown error at that line. One easy-to-miss detail: wrapping the *second* `await` in its own `return await` (rather than just `return fetchPosts(...)`) matters here specifically so the rejection surfaces **inside** this function's `try` block instead of escaping as an already-rejected promise the caller has to handle separately.

The single most common `async`/`await` performance bug: `await`-ing inside a loop when the iterations don't actually depend on each other.

```javascript
// Serializes — each request waits for the previous one to fully finish
for (const id of userIds) {
  const user = await fetchUser(id); // 200ms each, 10 ids = ~2000ms total
  results.push(user);
}

// Parallelizes — all requests fire together
const results = await Promise.all(userIds.map((id) => fetchUser(id))); // ~200ms total
```

Each `await` inside the loop fully suspends that iteration until the request settles **before starting the next one** — ten independent 200ms requests become a 2-second serial chain instead of one 200ms parallel batch. The fix is to **start** every request first (`.map` without `await`, producing an array of in-flight promises), then `await Promise.all(...)` once, at the end. This only applies when iterations are independent — a loop that genuinely needs iteration N's result to make iteration N+1's request has no choice but to stay sequential.

A common live-coding prompt — it forces you to demonstrate you understand settle-once and ordering, not just call the built-in:

```javascript
function myPromiseAll(promises) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const results = new Array(promises.length);
    let remaining = promises.length;
    if (remaining === 0) return resolve(results);

    promises.forEach((p, i) => {
      Promise.resolve(p).then((value) => {
        results[i] = value;        // preserve input order, not completion order
        remaining -= 1;
        if (remaining === 0) resolve(results);
      }, reject);                    // any single rejection rejects the whole thing
    });
  });
}
```

Two details that separate a correct answer from a naive one: **results must preserve input order**, not the order promises actually resolve in — hence writing to `results[i]`, not pushing. And rejection must be **immediate** on the first failure, not waited for — the second argument to `.then` handles that per-promise without needing a separate `.catch`.

`await` **looks** like a blocking call, but it isn't one — it suspends only the current `async` function, and hands control straight back to the event loop, which is free to run other code (other event handlers, other `async` functions, rendering) while the awaited promise is pending.

```javascript
async function slow() { await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 5000)); }
slow();
console.log("still runs immediately, UI is not frozen");
```

This is exactly why `await` is safe to use in UI code — it never blocks clicks, scrolling, or other timers the way a synchronous 5-second loop would. The one place this nuance actually bites: awaiting inside a loop that *should* run in parallel (previous pitfall) doesn't freeze the UI either, it just serializes network requests unnecessarily — the mistake is about wall-clock efficiency, not about blocking anything.

`Promise.race` is exactly the right tool for "give up if this takes too long," a very common real requirement `fetch` doesn't handle natively without `AbortController`:

```javascript
function withTimeout(promise, ms) {
  const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("timed out")), ms)
  );
  return Promise.race([promise, timeout]);
}

await withTimeout(fetchUser(id), 3000); // rejects with "timed out" if fetchUser takes > 3s
```

`race` settles with whichever of the two promises settles first — if `fetchUser` responds within 3 seconds, its result wins; otherwise the timer's rejection wins. Note this doesn't actually **cancel** the slow request — it just stops waiting for it; a real production version pairs this with `AbortController` to also cancel the underlying fetch and avoid wasted work.
